---
code: survival-rederivation-disagreement
when-fires: a SURVIVAL verdict the wall walk had already minted was not confirmed when re-derived through the independent reference model (`300:lane-sparing-rederivation`). Before a plan ships, every survival -- an elision kept past a RUNNING wall, the `--risk-faultless-skips` tier -- goes back through `plan::rederive::recheck_survival`, which translates production's own inputs (the wall's footprint hit-surface, the fact's backing member set, the resolver output, the selector dialect) into `dorc-sparing-reference`'s opaque token vocabulary and asks the model the same question. A wall the model will not spare demotes the site to the guard/run tier, and this is the disclosure. Fires ONLY when our two implementations of one algebra answer differently: a production bug, a model bug, or a law that does not decide the case. Never anything the book did. Sited on the demoted line; `{wall}` = which crossed wall the model stopped at, by ordinal.
why: the re-derivation is a runtime checker in the `verified-core-discipline` ladder, and its structure is DEMOTE-ONLY: `recheck_survival` consumes the already-minted `SurvivalWitness` by value and can only hand that same value back or refuse it, so agreement licenses nothing new and disagreement can only ever remove a survival (`271:rul-net-quality-u-curve`'s pass condition; the lexical fence `rederivation_never_mints_a_survival_witness` holds it). That is what makes every approximation in the adapter safe in one direction: a too-permissive model loses checking power, a too-strict one costs an elision, and neither can manufacture a survival, because the input set is production's own output. ERROR severity with a WarnOrDeny floor: the resulting plan is still valid and still safe -- the site simply runs, exactly as it would with the flag off -- but a checker firing must never be silenceable below Warning. SITED rather than spanless, unlike its solver-certifier cousin: a survival verdict IS about one line, so pointing at that line names the elision the reader did not get and attributes nothing to the admin's text. Fixture-routed, for the same reason the certifier's case is: no book can arrange for our own two implementations to disagree (`303:fnd-refusal-has-no-honest-trigger`). Remediation register (help) wanted: say plainly that this is Dorc's own defect and not the book's, that the plan is safe but carries one fewer elision, and that the published receipt preserves the decision's record for a bug report.
---
